# Who may sign commits in this repository, for `git verify-commit` and
# `git log --show-signature`.
#
# Commits made locally are SSH-signed (`gpg.format = ssh`), and SSH signature
# verification needs a file naming the keys to trust. Without one, git reports
#
#     error: gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile needs to be configured and exist
#
# which reads like a broken setup rather than a missing config. Point git at
# this file once:
#
#     git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile .allowed_signers
#
# This covers locally-authored commits only. Commits on `main` are squash
# merges committed by `GitHub <noreply@github.com>` and signed with GitHub's
# *PGP* key, which is a different mechanism entirely — verifying those needs
# GitHub's public PGP key in your keyring, and nothing here affects them.

